cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite both the software and the accompanying paper."
title: "Long-term Tracking of Individual Pigs Through Re-identification"
abstract: >-
  Livestock production can benefit from automated monitoring of animal health, welfare and productivity. However, continuous
  longitudinal tracking of commercial pigs using Computer Vision (CV) is hindered by their uniform appearance, the need to avoid
  physical identification tags in most commercial settings, and ongoing changes in appearance due to growth and environmental
  factors such as dirt. This study addresses longitudinal visual tracking of commercial pigs through an open-set Re-Identification
  (ReID) framework based on deep metric learning, designed to maintain the identities of visually uniform group-housed pigs over
  extended periods without relying on artificial or natural marks or other devices. A ResNet50 backbone was fine-tuned with a
  combined ArcFace and semi-hard triplet loss on an extended large-scale longitudinal pig dataset.
  To mitigate embedding drift, a multi-anchor voting algorithm was introduced to match daily cluster centroids against a rolling
  bank of historical representations of individual animal identities. A custom synthetic dirt augmentation pipeline was developed
  to prevent overfitting to transient patterns of animal dirt contamination. Performance was evaluated across three input modalities:
  Instance Segmentation (IS), Oriented Bounding Boxes (OBB), and Axis-Aligned Bounding Boxes (AABB). During an 18-day
  open-set evaluation, the multi-anchor framework reached a mean tracking accuracy of 88.0% with IS images, outperforming a
  sequential day-to-day baseline (54.6%). Importantly, this work demonstrates that open-set visual ReID can maintain individual
  identity continuity in visually uniform, previously unseen commercial pigs for up to 18 days, substantially extending ReID beyond
  short-clip identification towards practical longitudinal tracking. OBB performance was comparable to IS masks while offering
  lower computational cost, whereas AABB degraded to 51.4% attributed to background noise. Synthetic dirt augmentation proved
  essential for the tightly cropped modalities, as otherwise longitudinal tracking was reduced to 56.5% for IS images.
  Model interpretability using an adapted GradCAM verified that the network differentiated individuals based on stable anatomical
  traits, such as the head, shoulder, and rear, rather than transient artefacts. These findings indicate that ReID of visually homogeneous
  pigs is feasible without artificial marking or physical tags in comparable farm environments, representing a step toward
  non-invasive, continuous welfare monitoring in precision livestock farming.
